Verification complete. I checked the scoped diff (the `bound` timeout-fallback in `cmd_capability` plus the new `test_capability_without_timeout_still_attaches` test) against the current worktree files, traced the `cap`/`run_in` helpers and the `gh-cap-stub.sh` fixture, and confirmed the empty-array expansion `${bound[@]+"${bound[@]}"}` is safe under `set -u`, that the restricted-PATH test genuinely removes `timeout`/`gtimeut` while keeping `bash`/`git`/`head`/`tr` reachable via `/usr/bin` symlinks, and that the test discriminates old vs new behavior (pre-change code would print `branch`). The one genuine (deliberate but real) degradation is that probes lose their time bound entirely on systems with no `timeout(1)`, so a hanging `gh` hangs `capability` with no exit path.

{"findings":[{"severity":"low","file":"skills/pr-diagrams/scripts/pr-diagrams.sh","line":261,"summary":"When neither timeout(1) nor gtimeout(1) exists, the gh probes run with no time bound, so a hanging gh makes cmd_capability hang indefinitely instead of returning branch per the failure contract.","failure_scenario":"On stock macOS (no timeout/gtimeout), gh repo view stalls on a dead network connection -> capability never returns -> explain-pr-changes blocks forever, whereas the spec requires each probe bounded to PR_DIAGRAMS_PROBE_TIMEOUT with branch fallback.","fix":"When no timeout binary exists, either fall back to branch mode (safe side, matching the spec's probe-timeout contract) or implement a portable bound (run gh in background, wait with a timeout loop, kill on expiry) instead of running unbounded."}],"verdict":"approve"}
